  {
    "knowledge_point": 17592186059820,
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Express $\\sin\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ in terms of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x$. Enter both signed coefficients, including zero when needed.\n\n$\\sin\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)=$ {{field-1}} $\\sin x+$ {{field-2}} $\\cos x$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Use the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u-v)=\\sin u\\cos v-\\cos u\\sin v.\n$$\n\nThe fixed angle has exact values\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)=0,\\qquad \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\nSubstitute $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$, then simplify the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)&=\\sin x\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)-\\cos x\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\sin x\\left(0\\right)-\\cos x\\left(1\\right)\\\\\n&=-\\cos x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe requested coefficients are $0$ and $-1$, respectively. A negative coefficient goes inside its blank.",
